/// # The omp Plugin {#omp}
///
/// @see mim::plug::omp
///
/// [TOC]
///
/// CPU parallelism through [OpenMP](https://www.openmp.org): a fork-join parallel loop over contiguous chunks of an
/// iteration range.
/// `omp.outline` lifts each loop body into a closed kernel, and the `ll` backend hands that kernel to the OpenMP
/// runtime through the C wrapper `mim_par_for` in `rt/mim_omp_rt.c` (see @ref plugin_runtime), so the emitted
/// module has to be linked with `-fopenmp`.
/// With this plugin loaded (`-p omp`), `btensor` runs the leading output dims of every `map_reduce` loop nest as
/// one such parallel loop (see `btensor.outer_nest`).
///
/// ## Dependencies
///
plugin core;
plugin cps;
plugin mem;
import compile;
///
/// ## Operations
///
/// ### par_for
///
/// Runs `body` over `[begin, end)` with stride `step` in parallel.
/// Every thread receives one contiguous sub-range `[lo, hi)` of the iterations together with its own memory and
/// iterates it sequentially; nothing but memory is carried across iterations, and `body` must not use memory from
/// outside the loop.
/// The address space `a` is only ever `0` here; it keeps the body's type open, since the closed `Cn [mem.M 0]`
/// of a loop's exit block must not be fixed by an axiom's type before closure conversion sees it.
axm par_for: {m a: Nat}
           → [body: Cn [mem.M a, lo hi: Idx m, Cn [mem.M a]]]
           → [mem.M a, begin _end step: Idx m]
           → mem.M a;
///
/// ### launch
///
/// The outlined form of `par_for` (see `outline`): `kernel` names, by its symbol, an external function
/// `Cn [mem.M 0, mem.Ptr0 Env, lo hi: I64, Cn [mem.M 0]]` that reads the values the loop body captured from `env`,
/// which the `ll` backend passes through a pointer alongside the chunk bounds.
/// Naming the kernel instead of referring to it keeps it an ordinary external function for closure conversion.
axm launch: {Env: *, n: Nat}
          → [kernel: «n; I8»]
          → [mem.M 0, env: Env, begin _end step: I64]
          → mem.M 0;
///
/// ## Phases
///
/// ### outline
///
/// Lifts the body of every `par_for` into a closed kernel over an environment of the values it captures and
/// rewrites the loop into a `launch`.
/// A body whose kernel cannot be closed - it uses memory from outside the loop, or a type of a captured value
/// depends on a value of the enclosing function - runs sequentially instead, with a warning.
axm outline: compile.Phase;
